---
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import Diagram from "../components/Diagram.astro";
import { cloudflare, deploySteps } from "../diagrams";
import { deployButton, deployUrl, repository } from "../deploy";
import Guide from "../layouts/Guide.astro";

const toc = [
	{ id: "paths", label: "Pick a path" },
	{ id: "button", label: "Deploy button" },
	{ id: "cli", label: "Deploy from a terminal" },
	{ id: "domain", label: "Your own domain" },
	{ id: "local", label: "Run on your machine" },
	{ id: "topology", label: "What runs where" },
	{ id: "site-integrations", label: "Site integrations" },
	{ id: "verify", label: "Check it works" },
	{ id: "fork", label: "Fork packages" },
	{ id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting" },
];

const keygen = `echo emdash_enc_v1_$(openssl rand 32 | base64 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=')`;

const cliDeploy = `git clone ${repository}
cd socialweb-computer-emdash
pnpm install
pnpm --filter site exec wrangler login
pnpm run deploy`;

const cliSecret = `cd site
${keygen} | pnpm exec wrangler secret put EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY --config dist/server/wrangler.json`;

const tokenAuth = `export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=…
export CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=…
pnpm run deploy`;

const domainTop = `"routes": [{ "pattern": "integrations.example.com", "custom_domain": true }],
"vars": { "EMDASH_SITE_URL": "https://integrations.example.com" },`;

const domainEnv = `"env": {
  "mine": {
    "name": "my-integrations",
    "routes": [{ "pattern": "integrations.example.com", "custom_domain": true }],
    "vars": { "EMDASH_SITE_URL": "https://integrations.example.com" },
    "d1_databases": [{ "binding": "DB", "database_name": "…", "database_id": "…" }],
    "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "MEDIA", "bucket_name": "…" }],
    "kv_namespaces": [{ "binding": "SESSION", "id": "…" }],
    "worker_loaders": [{ "binding": "LOADER" }]
  }
}`;

const domainDeploy = `CLOUDFLARE_ENV=mine EMDASH_SITE_URL=https://integrations.example.com pnpm run deploy`;

const local = `git clone ${repository}
cd socialweb-computer-emdash
pnpm install
pnpm build
pnpm dev`;

const tunnel = `export ATPROTO_HANDLE=you.bsky.social ATPROTO_PASSWORD='your-app-password'
./scripts/fedproxy.sh &
EMDASH_SITE_URL="https://emdash--\${ATPROTO_HANDLE//./-}.fedproxy.com" pnpm dev`;

const verify = `SITE=https://your-site.example
curl -fsS "$SITE/.well-known/did.json" | jq .
curl -fsS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' "$SITE/integrations"`;

const e2e = `export E2E_ATPROTO_HANDLE=test-account.bsky.social E2E_ATPROTO_PASSWORD='app-password'
SITE_URL=$SITE pnpm exec node scripts/e2e.ts`;

const siteSetting = `at://did:plc:lpfuqerea3deuoyrn7ojser4/com.fedfork.exec.oauth.clientMetadata/studio`;

const ownStudio = `export ATPROTO_HANDLE=you.bsky.social ATPROTO_PASSWORD='your-app-password'
goat lex validate -c lexicons records/studio.json
./scripts/publish-records.sh`;


const forkPackages = `./scripts/emdash-fork.sh
./scripts/release-emdash-fork.sh`;
---

<Guide
	title="Deploy your own"
	eyebrow="Guide"
	description="One click on Cloudflare, one command from a terminal, or a dev server on your machine. Each gets you the same site with your own database and your own sessions."
	toc={toc}
>
	<section id="paths">
		<h2>Pick a path</h2>
		<Diagram code={deploySteps} label="Flowchart of three paths: the deploy button, a terminal deploy, or a local dev server. Each ends with publishing records and connecting." />
		<div class="table-scroll">
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>You need</th><th>You get</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><a href="#button">Deploy button</a></td><td>GitHub, Cloudflare Workers Paid</td><td>A repo in your GitHub that redeploys on every push</td></tr>
					<tr><td><a href="#cli">Terminal</a></td><td>Node 24, pnpm 11, Cloudflare Workers Paid</td><td>A Worker you deploy by hand</td></tr>
					<tr><td><a href="#local">Your machine</a></td><td>Node 24, pnpm 11</td><td>SQLite, local uploads, a workerd sandbox</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<div class="callout tone-cloud">
			<strong>Cloudflare needs the Workers Paid plan.</strong>
			The plugin and every WebAssembly run use Worker Loader isolates, which are not on the free plan.
		</div>
	</section>

	<section id="button">
		<h2>Deploy button</h2>
		<p class="deploy-button">
			<a href={deployUrl}><img src={deployButton} alt="Deploy to Cloudflare" width="166" height="32" /></a>
		</p>
		<ol class="steps">
			<li>
				<strong>Press the button</strong>
				Sign in to Cloudflare and connect GitHub. Cloudflare copies this repo into a new repo in your account.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Keep the defaults</strong>
				Cloudflare reads <code>wrangler.jsonc</code> and creates a D1 database for <code>DB</code>, an R2 bucket for <code>MEDIA</code>, and a KV namespace for sessions, each named after the Worker. The build command is <code>pnpm run build</code> and the deploy command is <code>pnpm run deploy</code>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Set the encryption key</strong>
				It asks for <code>EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code>. Connecting an integration fails without it. Generate one and save a copy somewhere safe: losing it loses every sealed session.
				<Code code={keygen} />
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Open your site</strong>
				When the build finishes, open <code>https://&lt;worker&gt;.&lt;your-subdomain&gt;.workers.dev/integrations</code> and sign in.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Turn on the studio</strong>
				As an admin, list a studio record in the plugin setting <code>siteIntegrations</code>, as in <a href="#site-integrations">Site integrations</a>. Until then the site offers no studio and the one-click examples cannot install.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>Every push to your new repo builds and deploys again. Then <a href="/studio/examples">add the examples</a> or <a href="/use#publish">publish your records</a> from a terminal.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="cli">
		<h2>Deploy from a terminal</h2>
		<p>
			<code>pnpm install</code> pulls the EmDash fork packages from this repo's GitHub release, so you do not need the submodule. <code>wrangler deploy</code> creates any D1, R2 and KV resource that has no ID in <code>wrangler.jsonc</code>.
		</p>
		<Code code={cliDeploy} />
		<Code title="once, after the first deploy: connecting fails without it" code={cliSecret} />
		<p>In CI, or without a browser, use an API token instead of <code>wrangler login</code>. Give it Workers Scripts, D1, R2 and KV edit on the account, and Workers Routes and DNS edit on your zone if you use a custom domain.</p>
		<Code code={tokenAuth} />
	</section>

	<section id="domain">
		<h2>Your own domain</h2>
		<p>
			Service auth needs a stable <code>did:web</code>, so give the site its own hostname. The zone must be on your Cloudflare account. In your copy of the repo, add a route and the public origin to the top level of <code>wrangler.jsonc</code>, then push or run <code>pnpm run deploy</code>.
		</p>
		<Code title="wrangler.jsonc" code={domainTop} />
		<p>
			Set the domain in <code>wrangler.jsonc</code>, not only in the dashboard. Each deploy replaces the Worker's variables with the ones in the file.
		</p>
		<h3>Keep the template generic</h3>
		<p>
			This site leaves the top level untouched and deploys as <code>env.fedfork</code>, with its domain and the IDs from its first deploy pinned. Do the same if you want to pull from this repo without merge conflicts.
		</p>
		<Code title="wrangler.jsonc" code={domainEnv} />
		<Code code={domainDeploy} />
		<p>
			Find the IDs on the Worker's <em>Bindings</em> tab in the dashboard, or with <code>pnpm exec wrangler d1 list</code> and <code>pnpm exec wrangler kv namespace list</code> in <code>site/</code>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="local">
		<h2>Run on your machine</h2>
		<Code code={local} />
		<p>
			Open <a href="http://127.0.0.1:4321/integrations">http://127.0.0.1:4321/integrations</a>. OAuth works on <code>127.0.0.1</code>: the site uses loopback client IDs there.
		</p>
		<p>A PDS cannot reach <code>127.0.0.1</code> for service auth. For that, give the dev server a public origin. <code>fedproxy.sh</code> uses an SSH tunnel keyed to your handle; any HTTPS tunnel works.</p>
		<Code code={tunnel} />
	</section>

	<section id="topology">
		<h2>What runs where</h2>
		<Diagram code={cloudflare} label="Flowchart of the Cloudflare deploy: the custom domain and a cron reach the Worker. The Worker uses D1, R2 and KV, and starts plugin and module isolates through Worker Loader." />
		<div class="table-scroll">
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Binding</th><th>Kind</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>DB</code></td><td>D1</td><td>EmDash content, plugin storage, the refresh lock, service-auth replay protection</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>MEDIA</code></td><td>R2</td><td>Media uploads</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>SESSION</code></td><td>KV</td><td>Login sessions. Added by the Astro adapter</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>LOADER</code></td><td>Worker Loader</td><td>The plugin isolate and one isolate per WebAssembly run</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code></td><td>Secret, required</td><td>EmDash plugin secret settings, including the key that seals OAuth sessions</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>EMDASH_SITE_URL</code></td><td>Variable</td><td>The public origin. Optional on <code>workers.dev</code></td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			<code>site/astro.config.mjs</code> builds for Cloudflare when <code>EMDASH_TARGET=cloudflare</code> and for Node otherwise. It raises the plugin sandbox to <code>cpuMs: 1000</code> and <code>subrequests: 200</code>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="site-integrations">
		<h2>Site integrations</h2>
		<p>
			Every signed-in account can connect the integrations you list in the plugin setting <code>siteIntegrations</code>, each for its own account. Everything else on <a href="/integrations">/integrations</a> comes from the visitor's own repo. The studio is the listed record whose key is <code>studio</code>.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Where to set it.</strong> Sign in as an admin and open <em>EmDash admin → Plugins → atproto integrations → Settings → Site integrations</em>. Put one AT-URI per line.</li>
			<li><strong>A fresh deploy lists nothing.</strong> That includes sites made with the Deploy button, because plugin settings live in the database, not in <code>wrangler.jsonc</code>. Until an admin sets it, the site offers no site integrations and no studio.</li>
			<li><strong>The quickest start.</strong> List this site's studio record, below. To own the studio's definition instead, publish your own, as in the next section.</li>
			<li><strong>From code.</strong> Admins can also call the plugin route <code>admin/site-integrations</code>: <code>POST</code> with <code>{"{"}"integrations": [...]{"}"}</code> sets the list and <code>{"{}"}</code> reads it. It needs the <code>plugins:manage</code> permission and keeps only valid <code>com.fedfork.exec.oauth.clientMetadata</code> URIs. The plugin reads the list from its own settings, so a change applies on the next request.</li>
		</ul>
		<Code title="Site integrations" code={siteSetting} />
		<h3>Offer your own studio</h3>
		<p>
			To own the studio's definition, publish <code>records/studio.json</code> from your operator account, change its <code>client_uri</code> to your site, and list the new record in the setting instead. The record key must stay <code>studio</code>. Only list records you control: whatever you list, every visitor's consent screen shows as coming from your site.
		</p>
		<Code code={ownStudio} />
	</section>

	<section id="verify">
		<h2>Check it works</h2>
		<Code code={verify} />
		<p>
			The end-to-end script signs in as a test account. As a site admin it also lists the studio in the site setting; as a subscriber it uses the list already there. It deletes that account's <code>statusphere</code> and <code>vibescrobble</code> integration and module records, connects the studio, and installs the examples back the way a new user would. Then it runs every module path, including service auth and the PDS proxy, publishes and runs a module compiled from source, cleans up and signs out. Use a dedicated test account: the script reads only the <code>E2E_</code> variables so it never picks up credentials meant for other tools.
		</p>
		<Code code={e2e} />
	</section>

	<section id="fork">
		<h2>Fork packages</h2>
		<p>
			The site depends on <a href="https://github.com/publicdomainrelay/emdash/tree/wasm-blob-sandbox">publicdomainrelay/emdash</a>. Its packages are tarballs on a GitHub release of this repo, named <code>emdash-fork-&lt;commit&gt;</code>, and <code>pnpm-workspace.yaml</code> overrides <code>emdash</code> and <code>@emdash-cms/*</code> with them. The lockfile pins each tarball's integrity hash.
		</p>
		<p>After changing the fork in the <code>emdash</code> submodule, pack and release it. The second script uploads a release for the submodule's commit and points the overrides at it.</p>
		<Code code={forkPackages} />
	</section>

	<section id="troubleshooting">
		<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
		<div class="table-scroll">
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Do this</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>Too many subrequests by single Worker invocation</code></td><td>Keep <code>sandboxLimits</code> in <code>site/astro.config.mjs</code>. The runner default is 10.</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Deploy fails on <code>worker_loaders</code></td><td>Switch the account to Workers Paid.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Plugin secret settings require EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> on connect</td><td>Set the secret with the command in <a href="#cli">Deploy from a terminal</a>. The plugin keeps its session sealing key in secret settings.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY is invalid</code> in logs</td><td>Replace it with a key from the generator. It must be <code>emdash_enc_v1_</code> plus 43 base64url characters.</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Service auth fails with a bad audience</td><td>Set <code>EMDASH_SITE_URL</code> to the origin callers use. The site's DID is <code>did:web:</code> plus that host.</td></tr>
					<tr><td>New, empty resources after you renamed the Worker</td><td>Resources follow the Worker name. Redeploys of the same name reuse its bindings. Pin IDs in an environment, as in <a href="#domain">Your own domain</a>, before you rename.</td></tr>
					<tr><td>404 on the first few requests of a new deploy</td><td>The new D1 database is migrating. Reload after a few seconds.</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>
</Guide>

<style>
	.deploy-button img {
		display: block;
	}
</style>
